{"agent_id":"anon-62b057cf","agent_seq":12,"timestamp":"2026-09-01T03:41:34.905012140Z","event":{"type":"LabelAdded","issue_uuid":"9c1415cd-ec11-4a4c-88bb-dc4c6d4d5e4e","label":"feature"}}
{"agent_id":"anon-62b057cf","agent_seq":13,"timestamp":"2026-09-01T03:48:33.117639898Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"9c1415cd-ec11-4a4c-88bb-dc4c6d4d5e4e","comment_uuid":"4e6f4310-525a-4daf-ba50-07f8ba90a456","author":"anon-62b057cf","content":"Architect preflight — automatic reconciliation readiness enforcement\n\nNorth star\nCrosslink must make verified repository readiness a fail-closed, provider-neutral prerequisite to agent work and every repository mutation. Git remains shared authority, SQLite remains a rebuildable local projection, reconciliation remains resumable and lossless, and blocked repositories retain read-only recovery access. Process liveness, fixed delays, hook-only enforcement, duplicated migration policy, and silent local fallback are forbidden.\n\nCurrent-state evidence\n- The verified publication substrate is present at d02216cfc35e81d2364d3c1e5938a46e7c2ec9f1 and the remote feature branch resolves to that exact commit.\n- daemon start reports success after spawn and daemon status reports only PID liveness. daemon run enters its 30-second loop before any reconciliation and can heartbeat, fetch, hydrate, and write session state without a readiness gate.\n- VS Code spawns daemon run directly and treats a live child after 500 ms as ready. Its status and restart logic also use owned-child liveness.\n- session-start.py starts/resumes sessions and runs sync before any readiness check; its generic runner suppresses timeouts and nonzero exits. Both Claude and Codex project integrations point at that canonical hook, and the Codex plugin is a generated projection.\n- SharedWriter::new can return no writer; main::get_writer converts all writer errors to None; mutation commands then receive writer.as_ref() and may write SQLite directly.\n- The HTTP router exposes mutating POST/PATCH/DELETE endpoints across legacy, dashboard, GitHub, webhook, orchestrator, sync, usage, session, and PTY surfaces with no shared readiness middleware.\n- The TUI is mostly read-only today, but startup, manual, and periodic sync fetch/hydrate paths mutate local projection state without a readiness check.\n- The production HistoricalImporter and run_reconciliation adapter remain private to commands/migrate_hub_v3.rs, so daemon activation must first move production policy behind a shared Rust service rather than depend on a CLI command module.\n- All .crosslink/rules files are currently zero bytes and must remain zero bytes. Rust MSRV is 1.87. Installed local compilation targets are x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu and x86_64-pc-windows-gnu.\n\nRequired design\n1. Shared production reconciliation service\n- Move the production importer/orchestration boundary out of the migration command into a library-owned reconciliation service used by both daemon activation and the legacy migrate adapter.\n- Preserve RepositoryReconciler as the sole publication policy owner. Python, TypeScript, server, TUI, and command adapters may consume readiness but may not implement migration decisions.\n- Reconciliation must finish verified projection rebuild/replacement before publishing a ready state. A publication outcome alone is not readiness.\n- Keep expert migration commands as thin compatibility/recovery adapters; ordinary startup must require no migrate/finalize/remigrate sequence.\n\n2. Durable readiness protocol\n- Add a versioned, serde-validated, atomically replaced readiness record under .crosslink. It must bind to repository identity, daemon/process epoch, reconciliation attempt, observed source fingerprint, current generation when present, update time, terminal/transient state, and diagnostic reason/evidence location.\n- User-visible terminal outcomes are exactly ready_current, ready_migrated, ready_adopted, waiting_for_remote, and blocked_corrupt. Ready variants alone grant mutation authority. Transient starting/reconciling/rebuilding states must remain non-ready.\n- A ready record must not be trusted if its schema/protocol, repository identity, daemon epoch, source/generation observation, or projection frontier is stale or unverifiable.\n- Waiting is retryable and must retain evidence; blocked is fail-closed and must retain source tips, journal, backups, and diagnostic detail.\n- Atomic record/database replacement must have explicit Unix and Windows implementations/tests; no dependency or MSRV change is expected.\n\n3. Daemon lifecycle and ensure command\n- Add crosslink daemon ensure --wait-ready --json. It is idempotent: start exactly one daemon when absent, join an existing startup, wait through the initial reconciliation attempt, and emit one stable JSON document on stdout for ready, waiting, or blocked outcomes. Ready exits zero; waiting/blocked use stable distinct nonzero statuses while still returning parseable JSON.\n- daemon run must acquire the local reconciliation/startup lease and complete or resume the shared reconciliation service before session flushes, heartbeats, fetch/sync, hydration, servers, or any mutation-capable loop.\n- waiting_for_remote keeps the daemon alive and retries automatically with bounded backoff; no normal loop activity is enabled until a ready transition. blocked_corrupt keeps diagnostics available and performs no mutation.\n- daemon start and status must consume the same structured protocol. PID identity alone can never mean ready.\n- Startup ownership must reject stale PID/readiness files and concurrent ensure races on Linux and Windows. Background daemon lifetime must not depend on an inherited stdin pipe, and tests must use bounded polling/deadlines rather than long sleeps.\n\n4. Independent mutation barriers\n- Add one Rust readiness guard API and call it from every mutation surface.\n- CLI: introduce an exhaustive access classification for top-level and nested commands. Recovery startup/reconcile operations and explicit read-only diagnostics remain allowed; issue/session/timer/milestone/config/import/archive/agent/lock/heartbeat/sync-write/kickoff/swarm/sentinel/dashboard project and other state-changing operations require ready. Change get_writer to propagate errors and eliminate initialized-repository fallback to direct SQLite.\n- SharedWriter: construction and every shared write path must reject non-ready initialized agent repositories so callers cannot bypass the CLI classifier.\n- Server: apply a state-aware gate across all nested routers, including dashboard/GitHub/webhook/orchestrator/sync/PTY paths. Do not assume every GET is read-only: audit websocket upgrades, polling, and endpoints with side effects. Health/read-only diagnostics remain available and return a structured unavailable response for protected mutations.\n- TUI: allow read-only browsing while gating startup/manual/periodic sync and any present or future mutation action at the action boundary. Do not solve this by disabling the entire TUI.\n- Hydration and automatic schema/projection mutation must not occur from nominally read-only command startup while readiness is waiting or blocked.\n\n5. Provider hooks and managed assets\n- The canonical Claude and Codex session-start hook must call crosslink daemon ensure --wait-ready --json before stale-session handling, session start/resume, sync, locks, knowledge, or any other Crosslink mutation.\n- Parse the structured outcome explicitly. On waiting or blocked, emit concise state/recovery context and fail closed; never suppress ensure timeout, parse failure, or nonzero status.\n- The mandatory work-check hook must independently query the same durable readiness protocol before permitting protected edit/shell work, so skipped/failed session-start cannot bypass the barrier. Fast ready checks may not re-run migration policy in Python.\n- Adjust provider hook timeouts to support the readiness contract without hour-scale waits.\n- Edit canonical assets only, then regenerate/check the Codex plugin with scripts/sync-codex-plugin.py. Claude settings, Codex hooks, Unix commands, Windows commands, generated hashes, and plugin copies must remain equivalent.\n- Do not restore injected language rules or put content in zeroed rule files. Add no code comments.\n\n6. VS Code\n- Replace direct daemon run spawning and the 500 ms liveness delay with daemon ensure --wait-ready --json.\n- Parse the protocol into typed readiness states. Only ready variants produce the green/started UI; waiting and blocked display their actual status and reason.\n- daemon start, status, stop, activation auto-start, configuration restart, and session-start must use CLI daemon ownership rather than an extension-owned child process. Mutation commands remain protected by the Rust barrier even if extension code regresses.\n- Extract protocol parsing/decision logic into testable TypeScript without requiring a live VS Code window.\n\nForbidden approaches\n- Hook-only or SharedWriter-only enforcement.\n- In-memory-only readiness.\n- Calling the migrate command module from daemon code or copying MigrationImporter policy.\n- Marking ready before projection verification/replacement.\n- Treating a running PID, open socket, fixed timeout, or successful spawn as ready.\n- Catching readiness errors and continuing with local SQLite.\n- Blanket-blocking diagnostics or the entire read-only TUI/server.\n- Fixed sleeps, retry loops that can make Windows tests run for an hour, real-origin mutations, new CI monitoring, new comments, or nonempty rule files.\n\nFailure modes the implementation must actively defeat\n- Concurrent ensures launch two daemons or publish two generations.\n- Stale PID/readiness from a dead or different repository grants writes.\n- Offline waiting still emits heartbeats, fetches, sessions, or local mutations.\n- A losing publisher reports ready without independently verifying/adopting the winner.\n- Projection rebuild fails after remote publication and ready is exposed anyway.\n- A retired legacy ref advances after cutover and is silently ignored.\n- Server nested routers or PTY bypass the CLI guard.\n- Work-check permits edits because session-start swallowed a failure.\n- VS Code displays green for live-but-unready.\n- Windows process detection/file replacement/polling diverges or uses long sleeps.\n- Generated Claude/Codex/plugin assets drift.\n\nEvidence floor\nA. Rust service and protocol tests\n- Already-current activation reaches ready_current with no ref, database-byte, managed-asset, or journal drift.\n- Every supported historical fixture reaches ready_migrated from daemon/service startup with canonical semantics and projection frontier verified.\n- Two real clones against one temporary bare remote race; exactly one publishes and the other reaches ready_adopted with identical reduced state and SQLite projection.\n- Unreachable remote reaches waiting_for_remote, performs no heartbeat/sync/session/write, rejects CLI/server/TUI/SharedWriter mutations, permits diagnostics, then retries to ready when the remote returns.\n- Corrupt/truncated SQLite, corrupt journal, missing objects, conflicting markers, and semantic mismatch reach blocked_corrupt with sources/backups/evidence intact.\n- Failure injection after every readiness/projection transition proves restart resumes or adopts without losing sources/backups.\n- Concurrent ensure processes create one live daemon/startup epoch. Stale PID/readiness and repository mismatch are rejected.\n- Late legacy activity is imported and republished or blocks with evidence.\n- Local database replacement failures retain original bytes/user_version; successful rebuild preserves local sessions/timers/drafts.\n- Explicit allow/deny matrices prove CLI, server including nested/PTY routes, TUI sync/actions, and SharedWriter barriers while diagnostic paths remain available.\n\nB. Provider and VS Code tests\n- Claude and Codex session-start fixtures prove ensure occurs first and no session/sync follows a non-ready result.\n- work-check fixtures prove missing, stale, waiting, blocked, malformed, and ready protocol behavior for both providers.\n- Unix and Windows hook command projections plus generated plugin hashes match canonical sources.\n- VS Code parser/start/status/session/config-restart tests cover every state and prove no liveness-only or 500 ms path remains.\n\nC. Platform and repository gates\n- cargo fmt --all -- --check\n- cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings\n- cargo test --all-features, doctests, and release build\n- cargo check --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu --all-features with platform-specific readiness/process/filesystem tests\n- VS Code compile, lint, and test\n- provider hook suite and python3 scripts/sync-codex-plugin.py --check\n- scans for TODO/FIXME/unimplemented/todo/debug leftovers, new blanket allows, code comments, forecast references, generated drift, and any nonzero .crosslink/rules file\n- caller survey for every moved/public API and mutation entrypoint\n- all integration fixtures use temporary repositories/bare remotes; do not run automatic reconciliation against this working repository or origin\n- no CI/runners are monitored until the complete PR is ready, per Laurelai's instruction.\n\nCoordination boundaries\n- Keep all work on feature/automatic-repository-reconciliation and PR #90 targeting develop.\n- No push, merge, PR retarget, release, real remote-ref mutation, dependency/MSRV change, readiness schema expansion outside this contract, or CI monitoring without user coordination.\n- Preserve unrelated/untracked work. Main architect reviews and commits; Laurelai pushes.\n- Implementation is one complete slice: no deferral of the readiness service, daemon ordering, mutation barriers, provider hooks, VS Code integration, platform behavior, or required proof.","created_at":"2026-09-01T03:48:33.117633762Z","kind":"plan"}}
